# panic_narrowing_report — RUNTIME trap test (WS1-131, §1K, R-076). A narrowing
# numeric conversion (Int64 -> Int8) is CHECKED, not a silent truncation: the
# value 300 does not fit a signed 8-bit type (range -128..127), so at runtime the
# narrowing guard fires `eav_panic` with SSR0012 and exits 134 instead of quietly
# dropping the high bits to 44. This is the load-bearing test that narrowing
# conversions never silently lose data.
#
# Correctness observation (verified): the program compiles and runs, then traps
# with `EAV PANIC SSR0012 narrowing-overflow` naming the op, row, target type,
# and operands (value=300, target width=8); the process exits 134.
# Expected: runtime panic SSR0012 (narrowing-overflow), exit 134.

PanicNarrowingReport is project
PanicNarrowingReport module examplesPanicNarrowingReport
PanicNarrowingReport target console
PanicNarrowingReport entry main

examplesPanicNarrowingReport is module
examplesPanicNarrowingReport path examples.panicNarrowingReport
examplesPanicNarrowingReport exports main
examplesPanicNarrowingReport purpose "Trip the checked-narrowing guard and report it"
examplesPanicNarrowingReport invariant "Traps via eav_panic; never truncates silently"

ExitCode is alias
ExitCode for Int32
ExitCode purpose "Process exit status"

main is operation
main out ExitCode
main async no
main purpose "Narrow 300 to Int8 and trip the eav_panic narrowing guard"
main invariant "Never completes: panics at runtime with SSR0012"
main let big immutable Int64 300
main let okCode immutable ExitCode 0
main do narrowCall
main return okCode

narrowCall is call
narrowCall in main
narrowCall invokes convert.toInt8
narrowCall arg value Int64 big
narrowCall out small Int8
